<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  SlideStage Lite — macOS Hardened Runtime entitlements.

  Notarization REQUIRES the Hardened Runtime, which Tauri's bundler
  enables automatically as soon as `bundle.macOS.signingIdentity` is set
  to anything other than `"-"`. Hardened Runtime is restrictive by
  default and blocks behaviors that the Tauri/wry stack legitimately
  needs, so we have to re-allow them here.

  Why each entitlement is present:

  - cs.allow-jit
      WebKit (which `wry` embeds) compiles JavaScript to native code at
      runtime. Without this entitlement the JS engine cannot allocate
      executable pages and the viewer silently dies on startup.

  - cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      WebKit's lower JIT tier uses pages that are not strictly
      "JIT-style" but are also not signed. Required alongside
      allow-jit on macOS 13+ for reliable webview boot.

  - cs.disable-library-validation
      Tauri can load embedded helper frameworks and plugins that are
      not co-signed by Apple. Without this the app refuses to launch
      because the loader rejects "foreign" code.

  - cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
      Some webkit child processes inspect DYLD_* variables for
      hardened-runtime-friendly behavior. Cheap to grant and avoids
      brittle "works on my Mac, crashes on yours" surprises.

  - network.client
      The web viewport may fetch external assets (CDN'd reveal.js,
      analytics-free CSS, etc.) when a deck explicitly opts in via
      `trustCapabilities`. Hardened Runtime would block outbound
      network from a webview without this.

  We deliberately do NOT grant:
    - cs.debugger / get-task-allow:
        Anything that lets external processes attach to the running
        binary. Notarization will FAIL if get-task-allow is true.
    - app-sandbox:
        SlideStage Lite intentionally runs without the App Sandbox so
        users can open `.stage` files anywhere on disk. The Sandbox
        would require a long set of `files.user-selected` /
        `files.bookmarks.app-scope` workarounds that are out of scope
        for the Lite product.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
